Durarara!! AU
Chapter 31
The girls were gone.
Mikado noticed first, the way a boy notices a room getting smaller. Anri’s back had already turned a corner. Rio had not looked behind her. That left three people under a late light that did not belong to Raira anymore.
Izaya’s smile had not gone with them.
“See?” he said, pleasant. “They listen to you. That’s rare. Most people listen to fear.” His eyes slid to Mikado. “You came toward the noise instead. I like that better.”
Mikado’s bag strap creaked in his fist.
“I— I heard there was a commotion. That’s all.” He glanced at Curtis’s ripped jacket, then at Izaya’s coat, then at the empty stretch of sidewalk where Anri had been. “Curtis-kun. We should go.”
Curtis did not look at the corner.
He looked at Izaya.
“He’s going home,” Curtis said. “You’re not collecting him like a stamp.”
Izaya laughed under his breath.
“Collecting. What an unkind word. I just wanted to walk a first-year through his extraordinary city. You already handled the extras. I handled the introduction. We’re practically a team.”
An engine cut the sentence in half.
Black motorcycle. Yellow helmet. The kind of arrival that made ordinary traffic remember it had somewhere else to be.
Celty rolled up to the curb and planted one boot. The visor turned from Izaya to Mikado to the torn Raira shoulder and stayed there a second too long.
Curtis felt the private channel tick, faint, like a hand on a table.
You sent the girls.
He did not answer it in front of Izaya.
He clapped once.
Sharp. Clean. A count-in without a band.
“So, Izaya. You like board games, right?”
Izaya’s head tipped, delighted and immediately suspicious.
“I like pieces that move when I touch them.”
Curtis opened one hand, palm up, as if the sidewalk itself were the board.
“Here’s mine.”
He set the pieces down with his mouth, not his fingers.
“One. You don’t talk about names that aren’t yours on this street. Two. The first-year walks. You want to follow, you follow me, not his door. Three. The rider isn’t your audience and she isn’t your trophy. Four.” A small, tired smile that did not reach both eyes. “You lie, the game ends and you get nothing. Same rule as last time. Friendship still isn’t on the table. Silence might be.”
Mikado stared at him.
“Curtis-kun— what are you—”
“You’re going home,” Curtis said, still watching Izaya. “That’s the only move you make until someone tells you otherwise.”
Celty’s gloves flexed on the bar. The visor ticked toward Izaya like a blade considering an angle.
Izaya looked at the imaginary board, then at the ripped jacket, then at the first-year who had walked toward a rumor and found a foreigner making rules.
His smile widened.
“A board with no money and no information. You’re offering manners.” He put a finger to his lip. “How rural. How noble. How fun.”
The yellow helmet did not move.
On a sidewalk that had already spent a pompadour,
Curtis Nightstar clapped and laid out a game with no dice.
Izaya Orihara looked at the pieces and saw a first-year
still standing on the board.
Celty Sturluson arrived as the third player who did not need
a mouth to change the rules.
Mikado’s hand closed on Curtis’s sleeve.
The ripped jacket fabric bunched under his fist. He planted his feet like the sidewalk was a hill he could hold.
“I’m not going anywhere.”
Curtis’s voice dropped.
“Don’t do this, Mikado. Not here.”
“No!” Mikado’s face had gone red in patches — jealousy, fear, the leftover of a boy who had spent the week watching rooms change around a foreigner. “I’m not useless and I’m not going to let you keep the spotlight!”
Curtis stared at him.
“What the hell are you talking about.”
The Black Rider stepped off the bike.
Yellow helmet. Leather. The empty space above the collar made the street feel narrower. She came up on Curtis’s other side, visor ticking from the fist in the jacket to Izaya’s smile.
Mikado swallowed and swung the only card he thought he had.
“Celty told me everything!”
The helmet tilted.
Celty flipped her phone around so the screen faced the three of them.
I never met this kid.
Curtis put his whole palm over his face.
“Mikado, you fool.”
Izaya made a small, delighted sound. Not a laugh yet. The inhale before one.
“Oh,” he said, sweet. “A name. In public. On a sidewalk. With me standing here. That’s generous.”
Mikado’s grip loosened a fraction. The color in his face changed meaning.
“I— she— the Black Rider—”
Curtis’s anger went inward, sharp, private, a line that did not touch the air Izaya was breathing.
You absolute fool. You weren’t supposed to mention her. The one at your apartment. The one we’re protecting. She’s connected to the Black Rider. And you just handed her info to someone hunting her.
Mikado flinched like the words had hit bone.
His mouth opened. Nothing useful came out.
Celty’s visor stayed on Izaya, then flicked to Curtis. The private channel toward Curtis ticked once, confused, not carrying the sentence he had just put in Mikado.
He used my name. I didn’t give it to him.
Curtis did not answer her on that street.
Izaya rocked on his heels, hands in his coat, happy as a man watching a piece move itself.
“Celty,” he repeated, tasting it. “So the first-year knows the Headless Rider’s given name and thinks he’s in a secret. And the transfer student looks like he wants to put him in a locker.” His eyes shone. “I haven’t even asked a question yet. This city keeps feeding me.”
Mikado looked at Celty’s phone again. At I never met this kid. At Curtis’s hand still on his own face.
The spotlight he had wanted turned into a lamp over a table he did not understand.
Curtis pulled his palm down. Mismatched eyes were flat.
“You’re going home,” he said out loud, for Izaya, for the street, for the part that could be heard. “Now. You don’t get another sentence.”
Mikado’s fist was still in the jacket.
It did not feel like courage anymore.
On a sidewalk with a board already laid out,
Mikado Ryuugamine played a name
he had no right to.
Celty Sturluson denied the meeting
in writing.
Izaya Orihara received a gift
he had not paid for.
And Curtis Nightstar put the real warning
where only the fool could hear it.
“No…”
Curtis’s head turned a fraction.
“The fuck you say?”
The cool mask slipped. Not all the way. Enough that Mikado saw the boy who had slapped a pompadour off the ground and called himself a freak in a girl’s head two corners away.
Mikado’s fist stayed in the ripped jacket anyway.
“Let me walk with you all.”
Curtis’s hand clenched.
For a second the street felt like it might go. Pavement. Light pole. The easy math of putting a first-year on the other side of the block and daring Izaya to comment. The air around his shoulders went tight the way it did before a trash can or a wall learned a new crack.
Then he let it out.
A long breath. Ugly. Tired.
“Oh, for fuck’s sakes.” He jerked his chin down the street. “Come on. Let’s all get out of here.”
Mikado blinked, like winning had not been the plan.
Izaya’s smile warmed.
“Group outing. I love field trips.”
Celty’s visor ticked toward Curtis. She rolled the bike off the curb and walked it, yellow helmet keeping Izaya in the edge of the glass.
The private line opened while their feet started moving.
We got a lot to talk about. I wasn’t able to tell you earlier. At the motel. Because, well—
Celty’s answer came quieter than her engine.
I know. You looked like you had more to tell me. But I’m not sure I was ready to hear it. Mostly because I’m still processing what Shinra and Mei have been doing. Then…
The then sat there.
She did not put the rest on the line.
Not I keep choosing your room.
Not your chest is quieter than his kitchen.
Not I asked for selfish and I meant you.
She filed it under smoke and kept walking.
Curtis did not push the unfinished sentence. He had just watched a first-year throw her name into Izaya’s lap. He could live with one more thing left unsaid for a block.
Out loud, for the board:
“Mikado stays on my left. Izaya stays where I can see his hands. Rider takes the street. Nobody improvises a speech.”
Izaya hummed.
“So many rules for a man who just sighed like a parent.”
“Keep talking,” Curtis said. “See what it costs.”
Mikado walked. Shame and stubbornness sharing the same step. He had wanted the spotlight. He had gotten a place in a line he did not understand, next to a rider who had never met him and a broker who now owned a name.
Celty kept the last word in her own head, where Shinra could not photograph it and Curtis could not have to carry it on top of a motel and a fool.
Then I started falling. And that is not a report I am giving you on a sidewalk with Orihara smiling.
On a street that had stopped being a school gate,
Mikado Ryuugamine refused to be sent home
and got walked instead.
Curtis Nightstar almost leveled the block
and chose a sigh.
Celty Sturluson admitted the motel had leftover truth
and stopped before love became another piece on Izaya’s board.
The line went to her only.
I found the girl you were chasing.
Celty’s visor snapped toward him.
What.
Black smoke hit his waist like a hook. The street yanked sideways. Mikado’s shout died behind them. Izaya’s delighted oh? got cut off by distance and an overpass.
She did not put him on the bike.
She put him in the dark under the bridge and pinned him there, smoke tight, helmet close enough that the visor was all he had for a face.
Talk.
Curtis did not fight the hold.
There’s a lot to unpack, so just brace yourself. The girl you’re chasing is Mika Harima. She had her face reconstructed to look like yours. Yagiri is involved.
The smoke flinched. Not weaker. Sharper.
How the hell do they know what my head looks like.
Curtis’s voice in the channel stayed even. The kind of even he used when a room was about to change meaning.
The better question is, how did they get your head. You told me someone jumped you and severed you from it. Do you have any fragments of who did it?
Celty’s gloves flexed on nothing. The helmet turned a fraction, like memory was a street she could still search.
I remember the weight leaving. I remember reaching for something that was already gone. I remember Japan after. Not a face. Not a name. Not a company. A thin beat. Yagiri. The sister. The boy with the bruise. The building that smells like a hospital that does not treat people.
The smoke around Curtis’s ribs tightened, then loosened enough that he could breathe without it being a favor.
Mika Harima. Raira. Anri’s missing friend. You found her. You didn’t tell me at the motel.
You weren’t ready. You said that. And I had her somewhere I promised not to point a rider at. He held the next line still. She’s not wearing you on purpose. Someone built her that way so a boy would look at her. That’s the part that made me pale at a bar.
Celty stood under the bridge with a city rumbling over her helmet and a name she had been hunting as a scar.
They have my head.
Not a question anymore.
Or they had it long enough to copy it onto a student. Curtis tipped his chin toward the light at the end of the concrete. Izaya is back there with a first-year who just used your name for free. We don’t have long. Fragments, Celty. Anything. A voice. A smell. A hand. Even a wrong detail helps.
The visor faced him.
No face. A room that was too white. Hands that did not shake. That is all I kept. Smoke curled, angry at the emptiness. If Yagiri has the rest, I am not asking Shinra’s kitchen. I am going through their door.
Curtis’s mouth tightened.
Then we do it without handing them Mika twice. And without giving Izaya the map.
Above them, traffic went on like no one had just named a head.
Under the bridge,
Celty Sturluson learned the chase
had a classmate’s name
and her own stolen face.
Curtis Nightstar finally put the motel leftover
into the dark where Izaya could not smile at it.
Curtis did not look away from the visor.
“Celty. I asked if you remembered. Because Mika told me who did her face reconstruction.” A short breath. “There’s not many in Japan right now with that slight of hand.”
Celty’s fists closed.
The smoke around his waist went hard enough to creak leather.
“No.”
Then smaller. Worse.
“No.”
Curtis’s voice stayed low. He did not dress it up.
“I’m sorry. And she was with him. Mei already knows.” He let the next part sit where it belonged. “Mika mentioned it before she went out cold. She heard a woman’s voice by him. Someone who seemed to know him.”
The helmet did not move.
Under it, the kitchen arrived anyway. A couch stain that had been cleaned too well. A desk that had been wiped like evidence. A helmet strap bought like an apology. Mei laughing in a doorway. Shinra saying we can’t, she could be home any minute like that was the only sin in the room.
Not this.
Not a student on a table.
Not a stolen face copied onto a living girl so a Yagiri boy would look at her.
Celty’s smoke shuddered.
He cuts things open. He has always cut things open. He cut me open. That was love in his mouth. The channel frayed. He would not— he would not put my head on a child—
“I didn’t say he enjoyed it,” Curtis said. “I said Mika named the hands. And Mei was in the room enough to talk.”
Celty took one step back. Then another. The hold on him loosened like she did not trust what her shadows would do if they stayed on his ribs.
Shinra.
The name in the link was not a question anymore. It was a house collapsing inward.
He knew what I was looking for. He let me ride around this city hunting a scar he helped carve.
Curtis stayed where she had put him.
“I wasn’t going to drop that on you at the motel. You were already drowning in the other lie.” His jaw tightened. “I’m dropping it now because Izaya is up the street and your name is already in his pocket. You needed the real enemy before you kicked the wrong door.”
Celty’s gloves shook once.
Then they stopped, which was worse.
Mika Harima is alive.
“Yes.”
Because he is good at his work.
Curtis did not answer that.
He did not have a kind version of yes.
Above them, a truck crossed the bridge and the whole underpass hummed like a held note.
Celty Sturluson stood in the dark
and watched the man she went home to
turn into a pair of hands
on a classmate’s face.
Curtis Nightstar gave her the name she had already said no to
twice — and the no did not make it untrue.
Celty’s fists were still closed.
The no had not left the underpass. Shinra’s hands were still on a classmate’s face. Mei’s voice was still in a room Mika had gone dark in.
When Celty spoke again, the channel was quieter than the smoke.
Thank you, Curtis. For not lying. For not sending me on a goose chase all around the city.
A thinner beat.
And thank you for indulging my selfish requests. Twice.
She did not list them. She did not have to.
The first one had been a blanket and a racing heart and hold me properly. Not polite couch distance. Not one arm free for the book and the exit. He had said no, it would not be selfish, and then he had meant it.
The second had been a motel mattress and a clock that still had last period in it. Do me this one selfish favor. He had said okay. He had stayed when Raira would have kept his cover.
Both asks had been her taking more than a courier was supposed to take.
Both times he had opened the door on purpose.
Curtis stood under the bridge with her smoke still ghosting his coat and did not make a joke of it.
“You don’t have to thank me for telling you the truth. That’s the minimum.” His jaw worked once. “The other thing… you asked. I said okay. That’s still not a debt, Celty.”
The visor dipped, then came back up.
It is to me. Shinra cuts and calls it love. You hear me ask for something selfish and you do not make me earn it with a lie or a cage. Smoke curled, unsteady. You found the girl. You named the hands. You could have pointed me at alleys for a week and kept your motel quiet. You didn’t.
Curtis’s mouth tightened.
“I almost did. Not the alleys. The delay. I told myself you weren’t ready.” He looked at the concrete ribbing over their heads. “Ready stopped mattering when Mikado said your name in front of Izaya.”
Celty’s gloves flexed.
Then we go back to them. After I put this somewhere I can ride with.
She did not say I love you.
She did not say the motel had ruined the kitchen.
She did not say the first selfish favor had already been a confession she kept off the channel.
She only stood in the dark with a boy who had refused to send her hunting a scar her own house had helped carve —
and called that refusal
what it was.
Not strategy.
Not a game piece.
The same answer he had given twice when she asked for something selfish.
They walked back toward the light at the end of the underpass.
Curtis kept his voice in the private line.
What do we tell these two. You just used your black smoke in front of Mikado.
Celty answered too fast.
We don’t tell them we had—
Curtis’s mouth twitched. Not a smile that reached anything.
Not what I meant, Celty.
The helmet ticked sideways. Smoke went thin, embarrassed in a way a visor could not hide from him.
…Oh.
A beat. Then the channel came back flatter, trying to be a courier again.
I thought you meant the motel. The blanket. The twice. I am not putting that on a first-year or Orihara. That stays ours.
I know, Curtis sent. I meant the part where you hooked me around the waist and vanished under a bridge like a magic trick. Mikado already threw your name. Izaya already smiled. If we walk back with no story, Izaya writes one for us.
Celty’s gloves flexed on nothing.
Then the story is I pulled you because he used my name and I wanted the transfer student who keeps ending up in my street. Not a confession. Not Mika. Not Shinra. Her pace stayed even. If Mikado asks what the smoke was, it was the Black Rider being the Black Rider. He already believes I told him everything. Let him sit in that mistake. Correcting it teaches Izaya.
A thinner line, almost dry.
And if Orihara asks why I grabbed you specifically… I do not owe him a reason I can hear you. He can think I am collecting another interesting human. He already thinks that about everyone.
Curtis exhaled through his nose.
So we come back annoyed, not informed.
Yes. Annoyed is cheap. Informed is how he eats.
They cleared the shadow of the bridge.
Up the street, two figures were still waiting where the board had been left mid-game — a first-year who wanted the spotlight, and a man in a fur-trimmed coat who had been given a name for free.
Celty did not look at Curtis when she finished.
The selfish parts stay off this sidewalk. The smoke becomes weather. The girl stays a scar they do not get to meet.
Curtis nodded once, out loud enough for no one.
“Yeah. Weather.”
On the walk back to a bad audience,
Celty Sturluson almost confessed
the wrong secret.
Curtis Nightstar put the question
back on the power
instead of the bed.
And the story they carried
toward Izaya
was only this:
the rider had grabbed him.
That was all the board was allowed to know.
Curtis kept it on the private line until the two shapes up the street got faces again.
Izaya doesn’t know I can hear you. Just keep using your phone to talk. You’re the only one who knows about my white smoke. Let’s tread carefully around the flea. He’s definitely not here for nothing.
Celty’s visor ticked once. Agreement. No argument.
Phone only. I remember. He already has my name from the first-year. He does not get your ears or your fog. A short beat. If I type, he thinks I am the same mute rider he has always circled. If I speak to you in the air, he files a new species.
Her gloves found the PDA like a reflex.
The white stays dead on this street. If you have to move something, you move like a tall student with a ripped jacket. Not like me.
Curtis’s mouth barely moved.
Copy.
And Curtis— the channel tightened, not soft this time, operational. He came for the boy. He stayed for you. That is not nothing. Do not play the board game like he is bored. He is hungry.
“Yeah,” Curtis muttered, out loud, cheap enough to be weather. “I got that.”
They reached the leftover pieces.
Mikado was still there, bag strap white in his fist, looking at the place under the bridge like it had eaten a minute he was owed. Izaya had his hands in his coat, smile already rebuilt, as if a rider dragging a foreigner into the dark was a complimentary appetizer.
Celty lifted the phone first.
Sorry. Business.
Izaya read it upside down anyway.
“Business,” he repeated, delighted. “With him. How intimate.”
Curtis stopped on Mikado’s left. Did not look at Celty. Did not look like a man who had just named Shinra under concrete.
“You two didn’t wander off,” he said. “Good. Less cleanup.”
Mikado’s voice came small and stubborn at once.
“What was that. The black stuff. She— she grabbed you.”
Curtis shrugged the ripped shoulder.
“Black Rider stuff. You’re not in it.”
Izaya’s eyes shone.
“Oh, but I am. I was standing right here when the weather changed.” He tipped his head at Celty’s screen. “Business. Name. Smoke. A transfer student who claps and makes rules. I feel invited.”
Celty typed again, slower, for the flea.
You are not.
Curtis let that be the air.
In the line, only to her:
He’s going to poke the grab until it bleeds. Don’t give him the motel. Don’t give him Mika. If he asks why you, we already have the story.
Celty’s thumb paused over the keys.
I know. Phone only. Fog off. Ears off.
Then, quieter, almost unwilling:
I still want to ride him off this street. That would also be something. I am not doing it.
Curtis’s jaw ticked.
Good. Hungry men who get thrown become legends. Hungry men who get bored make mistakes.
On a sidewalk that had been a board, they put the masks back on.
Celty Sturluson became a phone and a helmet.
Curtis Nightstar became a ripped jacket and a shrug.
Izaya Orihara looked at both and smiled like weather had just told him a secret anyway.